title: shell panel
tags: Panel [[Panel types]]
kind: reference
aliases: terminal output
summary: What the shell lines run in the workspace printed, read like a terminal: oldest first, one block per run, stderr in red, how a run ended.

Open it with `panel:open here shell` (<<key "panel:open here shell" "t s">>). A shell
line typed in the panel itself (below) or after `!` ([[Bash mode]]), a script started with `script:run`
([[Running shipped scripts]]) and a program started with `file:open-with`
([[Opening a file with another program]]) write their output here, not to the
[[message panel]].

It reads like a terminal, the oldest run at the ''top'':

* each run is a block headed by its command line and the time it started, followed by its output line by line, as it comes;
* what the run wrote on its standard error is in red;
* a run that failed ends with `[exit N]` in red, one stopped by `script:stop` with `[stopped]` (`[killed]` after `script:kill`); a run that succeeded adds nothing.

Two runs at once each keep their own block: a line goes into the block of the run that
printed it, not after whatever was printed last.

While the view is at the bottom it follows the output; once you have scrolled up into
older output, it does not move.

!! Typing a line in the panel

At the bottom of the panel, after `$`, is a line to type a shell command in, as in a
console — no need to go through the command input. Click it, or press
<<key "shell:focus line" "e l">> while the panel has the focus.

* `Enter` runs it exactly as `!` followed by the line would ([[Bash mode]]): in the background, with your shell configuration loaded (below), `%` standing for the selection, the run shown in the task bar.
* The directory it runs in is shown on its own line above the `$`, so that the field keeps the panel's whole width (see below). The field empties and keeps the keyboard, ready for the next line, and the view goes back to the bottom to show what it prints.
* `Tab` completes the word before the caret by asking `bash`, as in [[Bash mode]]: a single candidate is inserted; several extend the word to their common start and are listed above the line, until the next edit.
* `ctrl+p`, `ctrl+n` and `ctrl+r` recall what was run: the history is the one of the command input's bash mode, so a line run in either is recalled in both ([[Input history]]).
* <<key "editing:unfocus" Escape>> gives the keyboard back to the panels; the line typed is kept.

!! Where a line runs

Every shell line of a workspace — typed here or after `!` — runs in one directory,
the one shown above the panel's `$`:

* where the previous line's `cd` left the shell, as long as the selection has not changed since: `cd photos`, then `ls`, lists `photos`. A `cd` counts however the line ends (`cd x && false` still moves);
* otherwise the selection's folder: the selected folder itself, the folder holding a selected file — or, for a path that is gone, its nearest folder still there. Selecting something else moves the shell there, dropping the `cd`;
* with nothing selected, the root of the workspace's repository; with no repository, the directory the GUI was started from.

`Tab` completes a relative word in that same directory.

Each line is still a shell of its own: only the directory carries over, not a
variable set or a function defined.

!! Your shell configuration

Every shell line you type — here or after `!` — first loads `~/.bashrc`, so that
your aliases, functions and variables (a `PATH` extended there) are at hand as in a
terminal. A script (`script:run`) and a program opened on a file (`file:open-with`)
do not: they run in a plain `sh`, so that a script behaves the same whatever your
configuration, and prints nothing your `.bashrc` would. The file is set by
`shell-rc` in the GUI's `config.toml` ([[GUI configuration]]); a missing file is
skipped, and `shell-rc = ""` loads nothing and runs each line in a plain `sh`.

To load it as a terminal would, the line runs in an ''interactive'' `bash`: the guard
that ends a distribution's `.bashrc` in any other shell (`[[ $- != *i* ]] && return`)
lets it through, and its aliases expand. What a terminal-less interactive bash would
add is left out:

* its complaint about having no terminal is not shown — the `.bashrc`'s own errors and output are, at the top of the run;
* the line itself runs in a subshell: an `exit` prints no `exit`, keeps its status, and <<cmd "script:stop">> still ends the line (an interactive bash ignores the signal it sends);
* it runs in a session of its own, without the terminal the GUI was started from, which an interactive bash would otherwise try to take over.

Only `~/.bashrc` (or the file named) is loaded — not `/etc/bash.bashrc`, nor a login
profile. A `.bashrc` that is slow to load makes every line as slow.

<<cmd "shell:clear">>, or the panel's ''Clear'' button, empties the log. Each workspace
has its own, and none outlives the session.

!! What stays in the message log

The [[message panel]] keeps a line `$ command` for each run, so that its history still
says what ran and when, and the status bar still reports a failure or a stop — and so
the message log. Only the output moved.

!! Why a panel of its own

The [[message panel]] puts the newest line at the top, which suits a history of
one-line messages and reverses a command's output: the last line of an `ls` came
first. Output is read top to bottom, grouped by what printed it — so it has the
reading order of a terminal, in a panel of its own.
